Z||000000||BAM-BAM||04-23-2001||11:37 PM||||  Looking for some tips on installing my new carpet. I know that there alot of members that have done this, so any tricks or tips are appriciated.. TIA <P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000001||FieroLisa||04-24-2001||12:01 AM||||Well I'm assuming this is aftermarket carpet...but either way it shouldnt be too hard. To take it out, its simple: it just lays behind the center console and that back shelf carpet. Take out the seat too and you've got the carpet loose...you'll either have to take off the seatbelt reciever or slide it through the hole already in the carpet.<P>There is gray crap to keep the road noice out that needs to go under your new carpet, remember how it lays on there so you can put it back right. I guess aftermarket carpet can lay funny so you may have to adjust it to fit perfectly.<P>If I dont make any sense, let me know , Im not a good explainer :-)<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT- 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air,<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A><P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>Tarheels Fieros Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||04-24-2001||12:44 AM|||| Lisa, I have the removal part down to an art. It's the go back together part that always gets me. This is ACC carpet. It looks awesome and the mats I had made are unique. I guess what I'm looking for is where to start, how to proceed and general things like, lining up the seat bolts and the door trim screw holes and such. Here's my theory: Start by sweeping/vacuuming out the floor pan. The new carpet has the acustic backing, but I may use some of the old stuff just for additional deadening. Lay the carpet in the pan, square and check fit. Starting at the front, make sure all the contours are in place and smooth it toward the back. The console skeleton has retainer clips for the carpet, so I would assume that you would work your way back smoothing and cliping in while maintaining a good square appearance. I have no idea if triming will be neccessary. With the carpet smooth and flat punch a 1/2" hole (leahter or rubber punch) where each seat anchor bolts meet the carpet. This will alow any room for adjustment and still be hidden under the seat rail. After that it's seems pretty academic. Just keep working the carpet smooth, back and toward the door side, trim if neccessary, and individualy mark and punch each screw hole with a 1/4" punch. Use a wooden block to back the punch. <P>  I guess that about answers my question. If I am way off or anyone has a better and proven meathod. I'm all ears. I like to get the popular opinion on these matters from the folks who have already treaded these waters. Thanks Lisa for your input and an advanced thank you to those who may provide either support for my meathod or constructive critisism to the same. <P>Peace, B2 OUT ! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||6
